{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01LvbQqupUuigfGHHEd1ZPVn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good. Now I have all the context. Let me update the IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md to mark Item 5 as IN_PROGRESS, then create the SecurityConfig.java, SecurityWebApplicationInitializer.java, and update WebInitializer.java.\n\nFor Spring Security 4.2 with Java config and no web.xml:\n- `AbstractSecurityWebApplicationInitializer` registers the `DelegatingFilterProxy` (as a filter that delegates to the `springSecurityFilterChain` bean)\n- This must be separate from the DispatcherServlet initializer\n- SecurityConfig must be in the root application context (getRootConfigClasses)\n\n\n- I need to configure HTTP Basic auth with stateless sessions, CSRF disabled, and in-memory users with admin/admin123 (ADMIN) and user/user123 (USER) roles\n- GET requests to /api/employees/** require USER or ADMIN, while POST/PUT/DELETE require ADMIN only\n- Unauthenticated requests return 401 JSON and unauthorized requests return 403 JSON\n- Using `.antMatchers(HttpMethod.GET, ...)` syntax for method-specific matchers in Spring Security 4.2\n\nWriting security configuration... I see the tension here — the CLAUDE.md guideline says to write tests alongside implementation with a 70% test code target, but the IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md treats Item 5's tests as a separate deliverable in Item 14. Since Item 14 depends on both Item 5 and Item 8 (test infrastructure), I can't fully satisfy the testing requirement without completing Item 8 first. Given that both Item 5 and Item 8 are P0 priorities in the same group and Item 5 is listed first, I'll implement just the SecurityConfig for Item 5 now, get it compiling and committed, then move to Item 8 to set up the test infrastructure that Item 14 will need. Item 14 is a separate item. The structure acknowledges that test items are separate from implementation items.\n\nI'll implement Item 5 fully (SecurityConfig + SecurityWebApplicationInitializer + update WebInitializer), and compile/test.\n\nSince Item 14 requires Item 8 (test infrastructure) which isn't done yet, I can't write full security integration tests. I'll focus on Item 5 implementation and let Item 14 handle the tests later.\n\nFor Spring Security 4.2, plain text passwords work fine in `inMemoryAuthentication` without any prefix—the `{noop}` prefix wasn't introduced until Spring Security 5. Now I need to think through the authentication entry point strategy: `httpBasic().authenticationEntryPoint()` handles BasicAuth errors, but for form-based 401s I should also configure the entry point through `exceptionHandling()` to return JSON responses instead of the default WWW-Authenticate header.\n\nThe cleanest approach for a REST API is to set the same JSON entry point in both `httpBasic()` and `exceptionHandling()` so all 401 responses return JSON consistently.\n\nNow I'm writing the configuration code and updating the implementation plan.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2709,"cache_read_input_tokens":53452,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2709},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3e01a63f-0965-4a14-ba7a-31cb19b2c8f2","uuid":"5ebe5fea-c20f-4854-8786-9f57a16ca653"}
